<!-- Grahas Tab -->
<div id="grahas" class="tab-content py-10 px-5">
  <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto">
    <div class="mb-12 reveal">
      <span class="section-eyebrow">SECTION 01</span>
      <h2 class="section-title mt-2">GRAHAS — THE NINE PLANETS</h2>
      <p class="section-sub mt-3">
        The word
        <em>Graha</em>
        means "to seize" or "to grasp." These nine celestial forces influence all aspects of human experience — from the soul's purpose to the body's health.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div
      id="grahasGrid"
      class="grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-3 lg:grid-cols-5 gap-4 stagger"
    ></div>
    <div id="grahaDetail" class="detail-panel"></div>
    <p class="text-center py-10 text-sm" style="color: var(--muted-dim)">
      <i class="fas fa-hand-pointer mr-2"></i>
      Select a planet to view its full profile.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto mt-10 reveal">
    <h3
      class="text-xl font-bold mb-3"
      style="font-family: var(--font-display); color: var(--gold2)"
    >
      COSMIC MOTIONS
    </h3>
    <p class="text-sm mb-6" style="color: var(--muted)">
      Jyotish is read from an Earth-centered perspective. The table below separates physical astronomy from apparent zodiac motion so the difference is clear.
    </p>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
      <div
        class="rounded-xl overflow-hidden"
        style="border: 1px solid var(--border); background: var(--card)"
      >
        <div
          class="px-4 py-3"
          style="
            background: var(--card2);
            border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
          "
        >
          <h4
            class="font-bold"
            style="font-family: var(--font-display); color: var(--cream)"
          >
            Astronomical Reality
          </h4>
        </div>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="w-full text-sm">
            <thead>
              <tr
                style="
                  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
                  color: var(--gold);
                "
              >
                <th class="text-left p-4">Graha</th>
                <th class="text-left p-4">Body</th>
                <th class="text-left p-4">Rotation</th>
                <th class="text-left p-4">Orbit</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody id="astroRealityBody"></tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div
        class="rounded-xl overflow-hidden"
        style="border: 1px solid var(--border); background: var(--card)"
      >
        <div
          class="px-4 py-3"
          style="
            background: var(--card2);
            border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
          "
        >
          <h4
            class="font-bold"
            style="font-family: var(--font-display); color: var(--cream)"
          >
            Apparent Motion in Jyotish
          </h4>
        </div>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="w-full text-sm">
            <thead>
              <tr
                style="
                  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
                  color: var(--gold);
                "
              >
                <th class="text-left p-4">Graha</th>
                <th class="text-left p-4">Time in One Sign</th>
                <th class="text-left p-4">Full Zodiac Cycle</th>
                <th class="text-left p-4">Note</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody id="apparentMotionBody"></tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-xs mt-4" style="color: var(--muted-dim)">
      * The Sun's rotation varies by latitude because it is a gaseous body. Jyotish charts themselves are geocentric, so the second table is the more directly useful one for learning planetary movement through the zodiac.
    </p>
    <p class="text-xs mt-2" style="color: var(--muted-dim)">
      * Rahu and Ketu are not physical planets. They are the two intersection points where the Moon's orbit crosses the ecliptic, producing eclipses. Their complete nodal cycle takes approximately 18.6 years.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>
